#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail

cat <<'CHEATSHEET'
Bash Shortcut Cheat Sheet
=========================

Cursor Movement
- Ctrl+a : Move to beginning of line
- Ctrl+e : Move to end of line
- Alt+b  : Move back one word
- Alt+f  : Move forward one word
- Ctrl+xx: Toggle between cursor and line start

Editing
- Ctrl+u : Cut from cursor to beginning of line
- Ctrl+k : Cut from cursor to end of line
- Alt+d  : Cut next word
- Ctrl+w : Cut previous word
- Ctrl+y : Paste (yank) last cut text
- Alt+y  : Rotate through kill ring after yank
- Ctrl+d : Delete character under cursor
- Ctrl+h : Delete character before cursor (Backspace)
- Alt+Backspace : Delete previous word
- Ctrl+t : Transpose characters
- Alt+t  : Transpose words
- Ctrl+l : Clear screen

History
- Ctrl+p : Previous command (same as Up)
- Ctrl+n : Next command (same as Down)
- Ctrl+r : Reverse search history
- Ctrl+s : Forward search history (if enabled)
- Ctrl+g : Cancel history search
- !!     : Run previous command
- !n     : Run command number n (from `history`)
- !string: Run most recent command starting with string
- Alt+.  : Insert last argument of previous command

Process / Terminal Control
- Ctrl+c : Interrupt current foreground process
- Ctrl+z : Suspend current foreground process
- fg     : Resume suspended job in foreground
- bg     : Resume suspended job in background
- jobs   : List jobs
- Ctrl+d : Send EOF (often exits shell if line is empty)

Completion
- Tab    : Auto-complete command/path
- Tab Tab: Show completion candidates

Notes
- "Alt" shortcuts may require pressing Esc then key on some terminals.
- If Ctrl+s freezes terminal, run: stty -ixon
CHEATSHEET
